{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"c0644f80257d915ccc141f4d018152f95857943c7fa1ee841fab5d259248ddbf","slug":"chapters.02.causality-and-experiments","location":"/chapters/02/causality-and-experiments.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Causality and Experiments","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Ani Adhikari","given":"Ani","family":"Adhikari"},"name":"Ani Adhikari","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"},{"nameParsed":{"literal":"John DeNero","given":"John","family":"DeNero"},"name":"John DeNero","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-1"},{"nameParsed":{"literal":"David Wagner","given":"David","family":"Wagner"},"name":"David Wagner","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-2"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","CC":true}},"github":"https://github.com/data-8/textbook","numbering":{"title":{"enabled":true}},"source_url":"https://github.com/data-8/textbook/blob/main/chapters/02/causality-and-experiments.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/data-8/textbook/edit/main/chapters/02/causality-and-experiments.md","enumerator":"2","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"causality-and-experiments.md","url":"/build/causality-and-experi-3267dcae9cd039be1cbbf62bfa75a54e.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"“These problems are, and will probably ever remain, among the inscrutable\nsecrets of nature. They belong to a class of questions radically inaccessible to\nthe human intelligence.”","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"vlczZPHlAQ"}],"key":"QGNLuV0C11"},{"type":"text","value":" —The Times of London, September 1849, on how cholera\nis contracted and spread","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"QLx1qkrvw4"}],"key":"vS0pCL1RoR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Does the death penalty have a deterrent effect? Is chocolate good for you? What\ncauses breast cancer?","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"BBzGuRuRO6"}],"key":"M3gc57kLwL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"All of these questions attempt to assign a cause to an effect. A careful\nexamination of data can help shed light on questions like these. In this section\nyou will learn some of the fundamental concepts involved in establishing\ncausality.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"bWyYvHD4hR"}],"key":"GQyDtW3m22"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observation is a key to good science. An ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"UDBPVDHHX5"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"observational study","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"bOvZ1gGBnn"}],"key":"nTfMt5NiEO"},{"type":"text","value":" is one in which\nscientists make conclusions based on data that they have observed but had no\nhand in generating. In data science, many such studies involve observations on a\ngroup of individuals, a factor of interest called a ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"vP4nfxCYNr"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"treatment","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"ONpn0zXuiO"}],"key":"DeyjyTDdR7"},{"type":"text","value":", and an\n","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"LeE1xMh13E"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"outcome","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"Iifr60Rlyg"}],"key":"azXvkREGTk"},{"type":"text","value":" measured on each individual.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"NeaKHGIK2m"}],"key":"rmdmdVHhFL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is easiest to think of the individuals as people. In a study of whether\nchocolate is good for the health, the individuals would indeed be people, the\ntreatment would be eating chocolate, and the outcome might be a measure of heart disease. But individuals in observational studies need not be people. In a\nstudy of whether the death penalty has a deterrent effect, the individuals could\nbe the 50 states of the union. A state law allowing the death penalty would be\nthe treatment, and an outcome could be the state’s murder rate.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"UMiQTjE3Nj"}],"key":"JdWD1fDsyi"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fundamental question is whether the treatment has an effect on the outcome.\nAny relation between the treatment and the outcome is called an ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"KbvE5FrjBh"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"association","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"jbMfWBOeqD"}],"key":"IPANLY6wIS"},{"type":"text","value":".\nIf the treatment causes the outcome to occur, then the association is ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"YPziKtH3yJ"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"causal","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"bZDgF3aEfw"}],"key":"VYkjO2cTeh"},{"type":"text","value":".\n","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"owTqBCvoHq"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Causality","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"NeRQkgnrK8"}],"key":"YVSrmrpxgY"},{"type":"text","value":" is at the heart of all three questions posed at the start of this\nsection. For example, one of the questions was whether chocolate directly causes\nimprovements in health, not just whether there is a relation between\nchocolate and health.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"zu3YQ1Qvq8"}],"key":"pH5wj1bubm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The establishment of causality often takes place in two stages. First, an\nassociation is observed. Next, a more careful analysis leads to a decision about\ncausality.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"zQdBXmQdI6"}],"key":"mkPRpCRYjQ"}],"key":"T7fSStQ3fc"}],"key":"V1jKhw8gSR"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Another Kind of Character","url":"/chapters/01/3/2/another-kind-of-character","group":"Computational and Inferential Thinking"},"next":{"title":"Observation and Visualization: John Snow and the Broad Street Pump","url":"/chapters/02/1/observation-and-visualization-john-snow-and-the-br","group":"Computational and Inferential Thinking"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}